<UserControl xmlns="https://github.com/avaloniaui"
             xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml"
             x:Class="TanMenu.Wpf.Views.Settings.BehaviorCard">
  <!-- Six rows inside one Aero group box: an icon, a title + secondary line, and the control trailing —
       the row spec's base variant, six times. The row shape is Round 5, item 3's and is unchanged;
       the container was a Windows 11 setting card until Round 7, Task 7 deleted its fill and
       hairline and Task 9 made it a Windows 7 property-page group. This comment opened "Six
       Windows 11 Settings-style rows" until Round 7, Task 10. See the "row spec's shared classes"
       block in Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml for the anatomy and for what each class carries, and
       AppearanceCard's code-behind for the full ReloadFromConfig contract every settings card group
       follows.

       Round 5, item 3 rebuilt two of these six. 固定到任务栏 and 热键 used to wrap their Grid in a
       StackPanel and hang their status/help line BELOW it, indented by a hand-written Margin="30,8,0,0",
       on the argument that their control "needs more room than the trailing column offers". That
       argument does not survive measurement: both controls leave over 350 DIP of text column even at the
       app's documented minimum window width. What the arrangement did produce was a 14.00 and a 14.67
       DIP hole between each row's title and its own text — the below-row margin plus the slack under a
       bare, vertically centred title in a grid row sized by a 32 DIP control — and an icon sitting at a
       different y from every other row on the page. Both lines are now the header's own subtitle, which
       is where the spec puts a line that describes the row rather than a value the row holds, and both
       cards are ordinary two-line rows.

       ── ROUND 8, TASK 5: THE FOUR TRAILING TOGGLES ARE CHECK BOXES ────────────────────────────────
       Windows 7 has no toggle-switch control — the round's own Windows 7 survey found it in none of the
       UX Guide's articles and in no part/state class of parts-and-states — so the pill this row shipped
       with was an anachronism with a real counterpart, and Round 7's review had already flagged it as
       one. PUBLISHED, ctrl-check-boxes: "check boxes should be used only to toggle an option on or
       off", which is precisely what these four rows do. Nothing else about the rows moves:
       the handlers keep their names and their event, because IsCheckedChanged is declared on
       ToggleButton (Avalonia.Controls.Primitives.ToggleButton, decompiled 12.1.0) and both CheckBox and
       ToggleSwitch inherit it, and every IsChecked read in the code-behind reads the same property on
       the same base class. The x:Names keep the …Toggle suffix so the draft/commit call sites and the
       design's own verification steps still name the same elements.

       WHAT WENT WITH THEM: Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml's ToggleSwitch.tm-card-toggle, whose two jobs
       both lose their subject rather than moving. (a) OnContent/OffContent do not exist on a CheckBox —
       there is one Content and it is unset here — so there is no untranslated English "On"/"Off" left
       to blank. (b) The negative right margin compensated for a hard 12 DIP spacer ColumnDefinition in
       the ToggleSwitch template that no /template/ selector can reach; the vendored Aero CheckBox
       (Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/CheckBox.axaml) has an "Auto,*" grid instead, whose ContentPresenter
       carries IsVisible="{TemplateBinding Content, Converter=IsNotNull}", so a contentless box measures
       its 13 DIP glyph and nothing else. Measured on this machine at 150 % on a build with this task
       and a build without — UIA BoundingRectangle for the bounds, a PrintWindow row scan across each
       control's centre for the ink — on all four rows:

         element bounds   102 x 51 physical  ->  20 x 20   (68 x 34 DIP -> 13.33 x 13.33)
         element RIGHT    x = 4365           ->  x = 4347
         last INK column  x = 4346           ->  x = 4346  (UNCHANGED)

       (One of the four reads 20 x 19: UIA rounds an integral rectangle out of a 13.33 DIP box.) 4346 is
       PinTaskbarBtn's last ink column in the same capture; HotkeyClearBtn's is 4345, one physical pixel
       earlier, which is that button's own width rounding and is true before and after. The toggle only
       reached that column by overhanging its own bounds by the 12 DIP of empty presenter; the check box
       reaches it with its bounds, which is why deleting the class costs nothing.

       THE ROW'S TITLE STAYS WHERE IT IS. A check box carries its own label and the published end state
       is that label ON the box (ctrl-check-boxes) — that is Round 8, Task 6's move, together with the
       row grammar it belongs to, because these five titles are NAMED elements this file's code-behind
       writes and deleting one without rewriting ReloadFromConfig in the same commit is a compile break.
       Until then each box takes its accessible name from its own title through
       AutomationProperties.LabeledBy: Avalonia's ControlAutomationPeer.GetNameCore falls back to the
       LabeledBy peer's name when AutomationProperties.Name is unset (decompiled 12.1.0), so the name is
       the title's live text and there is no second copy of the string to keep in step. Nothing is
       duplicated on screen either — the box has no visible label of its own yet. It is a name GAINED
       rather than preserved: measured on the build before this task, all four ToggleSwitches reported
       UIA Name='' (ContentControlAutomationPeer's fallbacks are the presenter's TextBlock and
       Content?.ToString(), and a blanked toggle has neither), i.e. the rows were nameless to a screen
       reader. Task 6 moves the label onto the box and takes these four attributes off with the titles. -->
  <HeaderedContentControl Classes="tm-group-box" Theme="{StaticResource GroupBox}">
    <HeaderedContentControl.Header>
      <TextBlock x:Name="GroupHead" Classes="tm-group-head" />
    </HeaderedContentControl.Header>
    <StackPanel>

      <Border Classes="tm-card">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xED1A;" />
          <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
            <TextBlock x:Name="AutoCloseTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
            <TextBlock x:Name="AutoCloseSub" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
          </StackPanel>
          <CheckBox Grid.Column="2" x:Name="AutoCloseToggle" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding #AutoCloseTitle}"
                    IsCheckedChanged="OnAutoCloseChanged" />
        </Grid>
      </Border>

      <Border Classes="tm-card">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xE718;" />
          <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
            <TextBlock x:Name="TopMostTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
            <TextBlock x:Name="TopMostSub" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
          </StackPanel>
          <CheckBox Grid.Column="2" x:Name="TopMostToggle" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding #TopMostTitle}"
                    IsCheckedChanged="OnTopMostChanged" />
        </Grid>
      </Border>

      <Border Classes="tm-card">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xE7E8;" />
          <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
            <TextBlock x:Name="AutoStartTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
            <TextBlock x:Name="AutoStartSub" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
          </StackPanel>
          <CheckBox Grid.Column="2" x:Name="AutoStartToggle" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding #AutoStartTitle}"
                    IsCheckedChanged="OnAutoStartChanged" />
        </Grid>
      </Border>

      <Border Classes="tm-card">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xE840;" />
          <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
            <TextBlock x:Name="PinTaskbarTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
            <!-- The pin state IS this row's description — "not pinned yet", "already pinned", "pinning
                 needs a manual step" — so it is the subtitle, not a value line and not a below-row block.
                 The code-behind rewrites it from one of eight AppLanguage strings — five in
                 ApplyTaskbarPinState's switch, three more in the click handlers. None of the eight
                 contains a hard "\n", which is load-bearing rather than incidental: a
                 tm-card-sub renders an explicit break as a second line at EVERY window width, so one would
                 put this row's header at three lines and move its icon and its button off the y invariant
                 I1 centres every other row on. PinToTaskbarFailed carried one until this was written and
                 carries the comment saying why it may not; SettingsRowTests guards all eight.
                 Long-but-unbroken wording still wraps when the window narrows, the same as every other
                 row's subtitle, which is what tm-card-sub's Wrap is for. -->
            <TextBlock x:Name="PinTaskbarStatusText" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
          </StackPanel>
          <!-- The only button on this row. Its Theme is now STATIC, and Round 7, Task 3 is what made it
               so: TmAccentButton was deleted with the arrival of the Aero control themes (Windows 7 had
               no accent-filled default button), and the run-time Theme swap in ApplyTaskbarPinState went
               with it, because both of its branches had become TmButton.

               What the swap existed for still holds, and is still delivered — by IsEnabled alone. Round
               3, Task 3 (user item 8): once pinned there is no affirmative action left to offer, only a
               completed state to report, and a disabled TmAccentButton still painted a dimmed BLUE
               swatch (its ^:disabled rule only lowers Opacity; it never repainted Background off
               AccentDefaultBrush), which read as a live call to action, just quieter. A disabled
               TmButton has no accent fill to dim in the first place, so "done" now falls out of
               IsEnabled="false" — set by ApplyTaskbarPinState from the live COM pin query — instead of
               out of a second theme. One fewer moving part for the same rendering.

               The static Theme is also what makes the first frame of a process defensible.
               OptionsWindow's constructor calls ReloadFromConfig (hence ApplyTaskbarPinState) through
               ApplyText while this card tree is still detached from the logical tree — it was
               SettingsPage's constructor until Round 8, Task 2 and the hazard is identical, since a
               TabControl only realises the selected tab's content and this card sits on the second tab —
               and there FindResource cannot reach Application.Styles at all; a Button with no Theme and
               no Classes would have rendered as stock Fluent for that frame. It renders as TmButton
               instead, which is what it renders as afterwards too. -->
          <Button Grid.Column="2" x:Name="PinTaskbarBtn" Theme="{StaticResource TmButton}"
                  Click="OnPinTaskbarClick" VerticalAlignment="Center" />
        </Grid>
      </Border>

      <Border Classes="tm-card">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xE765;" />
          <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
            <TextBlock x:Name="HotkeyEnabledTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
            <TextBlock x:Name="HotkeyEnabledSub" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
          </StackPanel>
          <CheckBox Grid.Column="2" x:Name="HotkeyEnabledToggle" VerticalAlignment="Center"
                    AutomationProperties.LabeledBy="{Binding #HotkeyEnabledTitle}"
                    IsCheckedChanged="OnHotkeyEnabledChanged" />
        </Grid>
      </Border>

      <Border Classes="tm-card">
        <Grid ColumnDefinitions="Auto,*,Auto">
          <TextBlock Grid.Column="0" Classes="tm-icon tm-card-icon" Text="&#xE92E;" />
          <StackPanel Grid.Column="1" Classes="tm-card-text">
            <TextBlock x:Name="HotkeyTitle" Classes="tm-card-title" />
            <!-- The help line tells the user how to work the box beside it, so it is this row's
                 subtitle. Moving it out from under the Grid moved the box it points at from above the
                 sentence to beside it, so AppLanguage's HotkeyHelp was reworded from 上方/"above" to
                 右侧/"on the right" in the same commit — the same correction PinToTaskbarNotAllowed and
                 PinToTaskbarUnsupported needed for their own button. -->
            <TextBlock x:Name="HotkeyHelpText" Classes="tm-card-sub" />
          </StackPanel>
          <StackPanel Grid.Column="2" Classes="tm-card-actions">
            <!-- Key capture is wired in code with a TUNNELING KeyDown handler (see the ctor): a
                 bubbling handler would run after the TextBox has already consumed the key.

                 tm-keycap now carries only this box's SIZE and PADDING; its paint is the theme's. It was
                 once the ONE control on the whole 设置 page with no Classes and no Theme, so in every state
                 it fell through to the stock text box — Fluent's then — whose disabled fill was a different
                 grey from the one every disabled button beside it used, i.e. two disabled vocabularies on
                 one page. Four TextBox.tm-keycap /template/ rules in Themes/Shell/Launcher.axaml existed
                 to fix exactly that, and Round 7 Task 7 DELETED all four: every control on this page is a
                 vendored Aero theme now — this box, the 每列个数 NumericUpDown beside it, the three
                 ComboBoxes and the buttons — so "one disabled vocabulary" is true by construction, and
                 re-pointing the rules would have repainted a part with the value it already had. The box
                 shows Themes/Aero/ControlStyles/TextBox.axaml's own four states, and the focus signal that
                 says "I am listening" changed hands from a hand-painted accent hairline to Aero's own focus
                 border over its focus fill. Launcher.axaml's surviving TextBox.tm-keycap block records the
                 deletion and its reasoning; the retired before/after measurement went with the rules. -->
            <TextBox x:Name="HotkeyBox" Classes="tm-keycap" IsReadOnly="True" />
            <!-- Clearing a hotkey is a reset, not a forward-moving action, so this button never
                 qualified for the accent treatment even though it is the row's only button. The
                 criterion is moot now: Round 7, Task 3 deleted TmAccentButton along with the rest of
                 the accent-filled default button (Windows 7 had none), leaving TmButton the only
                 settings-card button theme there is. Kept because it records WHY this row never
                 changed when the two buttons that did carry the accent were moved. -->
            <Button x:Name="HotkeyClearBtn" Theme="{StaticResource TmButton}"
                    Click="OnHotkeyClearClick" />
          </StackPanel>
        </Grid>
      </Border>
    </StackPanel>
  </HeaderedContentControl>
</UserControl>
